$DIR/main.p:11:7: mu.Unlock used as a func value releases a lock wherever it is called, and a function called through a func value or a method value counts as called with no lock held, so the lock cannot be shown held there
    A lock is released where the code that releases it does not hold it. Every
    Unlock or RUnlock of a sync.Mutex, a sync.RWMutex or an rt.Lock, and every
    Wait(l) of a sync.Cond, must release a lock held on every path to the call,
    in write mode for Unlock and Wait and in read mode for RUnlock. A lock the
    compiler cannot name cannot be shown held: one reached through an interface
    such as sync.Locker, a func value or a method value, a pointer loaded from
    memory, or an array element with a variable index. A task, a scoped task and
    an interrupt handler start with no lock held, so none of them can release a
    lock that another party took.
    Release the lock in the function that took it, on every path, and name it
    the same way at both calls: copy a pointer into a local once (l :=
    &locks[i]) and use the local for both. To let another task end the work,
    send it a message on a channel instead of handing it a locked lock.
    Go leaves data races to a run-time detector that a program must be run
    under, and a racing Go program can see values half written. Plutonium tasks
    run in parallel with no such detector, so safe code must be free of data
    races, and the compiler checks it from the program itself, with no
    annotations.
    (see "Data race freedom" in the Plutonium spec, section 19.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3504" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:11:7: mu is read here without an atomic operation, but other tasks change it with atomic operations
    Every write to mu that another task may run at the same time holds atomic
    operations: pluto/sync/mutex.p:86 in (*Mutex).Unlock (the task started by
    task work at main.p:21). So the compiler infers that atomic operations
    guards mu, and every read must hold it too: a read without it may run while
    a write is half done.
    Here the task started by task work at main.p:20 reads it at main.p:11 in
    work with no lock held.
    Go leaves data races to a run-time detector that a program must be run
    under, and a racing Go program can see values half written. Plutonium tasks
    run in parallel with no such detector, so safe code must be free of data
    races, and the compiler checks it from the program itself, with no
    annotations.
    Read it with the atomic type's Load method, as the writes use atomic
    operations
    (see "Data race freedom" in the Plutonium spec, section 19.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3503" for a longer explanation)
